#{[17592186069874 1029 "The **quadratic formula** is a formula that we can use to compute the solution of a quadratic equation.\n\nGiven a quadratic equation in standard form\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nthe formula for the solutions to this equation is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic formula is derived using a method called *completing the square*. You'll learn more about this method and how it's used to derive the quadratic formula in future lessons.\n\nTo see how applying the quadratic formula works in practice, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\nx^2-2x-15=0\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of this quadratic equation are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}a=1}, \\qquad {\\color{red}b=-2}, \\qquad {\\color{purple}c=-15}\n$$\n\nTo compute the solution, we substitute the above values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(-15)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{64}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± 8}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 4 = 5 \\\\\nx &= 1 - 4 =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our quadratic equation are $x=-3$ and $x=5$."] [17592186069875 1029 "Suppose that we want to plot the curve $y=x^2$. We start by making a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y=x^2$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWe can now plot the graph using the $(x,y)$ values in our table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/453/Source/Images/2f2dda83f835794169c7c92b118013d9.png)\n\nThis curve's shape is known as a **parabola**, and the **minimum point** at $(0,0)$ is known as the **vertex** of the parabola."] [17592186069876 1029 "Sometimes, the union of two intervals can simplify into a single interval. For example, consider the union\n\n$$\n(-3,2) \\cup (0,4)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these two intervals on number lines, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/458/Source/Images/795726afbfc07d0a5e8c7a9deaa29b27.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/458/Source/Images/c2d115e3f6219ba83853a4ca13f0f215.png)\n\nThe union of the two intervals consists of all the points which are contained within at least one of the intervals. So, to graph the union of the two intervals, we take the graphs of the individual intervals and lay them on top of each other.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/458/Source/Images/c86c723ab93efdc3514caea6f2c42f4d.png)\n\nThis graph represents the interval $(-3,4)$. So, the union $(-3,2) \\cup (0,4)$ simplifies to the interval $(-3,4)$."] [17592186069877 1029 "The standard algorithm for multiplication works in the same way when multiplying three-digit numbers by three-digit numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute $237 \\times 142$. We start by writing it in the usual way:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ \\cdot & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}474 + 9, 480 + 23, 700 =\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}237 \\cdot 2 = 4740000000\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens. Remember that when multiplying the tens, the result will always end in a zero.\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 0 \\mid 237 \\cdot 40 = 9, 48000000\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the hundreds. Since a hundred ($100$) has two zeros at the end, the result will end in *two* zeros! So, we write these first:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}237 \\cdot 100 = 23, 700000\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed to multiply the rest of the hundreds as usual.\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}237 \\cdot 100 = 23, 700000\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ + & 9\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ + & 2\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 4 \\mid 474 + 9, 480 + 23, 700 =; = 33, 654\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n237 \\times 142 = 33, 654\n$$"] [17592186069878 1029 "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of\n\n$$\ny=(x-h)^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(h,k)$."] [17592186069879 1029 "The graph of $y=(x-3)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$, but shifted $3$ units to the *right* along the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/453/Source/Images/e8efe5bf306cb62dfc639f46cd7e2514.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=(x+2)^2\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard $y=x^2$ parabola after it's been shifted $2$ units to the *left*:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/453/Source/Images/831dd947a86373ef66d240665d7145cd.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0)$.\n\n**Watch Out!** It's easy to confuse the translations.\n\n- For $y=(x-3)^2$, we subtract $3$ from $x$ inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the right, *not* to the left (even though it seems like it should be the other way around).\n- Likewise, for $y=(x+2)^2$, we add $2$ to $x$ inside the parentheses, and the parabola moves to the left, *not* to the right."] [17592186069880 1029 "Other perfect square trinomials can be factored as the square of a difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} - 2ab + b^{2} &= (a - b)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's factor the expression $9x^2-24x+16$ using the square of difference formula. We perform the following steps:\n\n1. Write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n$9x^{2} - 24x + 16|=; (3x)^{2} - 24x + (4)^{2}$\n2. Factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(3x)^{2} - 24x + (4)^{2}|=; (3x)^{2} - 2(12x) + (4)^{2}$\n3. Write the middle parentheses $(12x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}4}$:\n$(3x)^{2} - 2(12x) + (4)^{2}|=; (3x)^{2} - 2(3x \\cdot 4) + (4)^{2}$\n4. Factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula. In this case, we have $a=3x$ and $b=4$:\n$(3x)^{2} - 2(3x \\cdot 4) + (4)^{2}|=; (3x - 4)^{2}$\n\n**Note:** Once again, we note that this method works *only* when the trinomial is a perfect square! It will *not* work for every trinomial."] [17592186069881 1029 "The **Pythagorean theorem**, also known as Pythagoras' theorem, is an important theorem related to right-angled triangles. Let's consider the right triangle depicted below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/433/Source/Images/eb7f67939f040e5f8413273d3cc0ca84.png)\n\nThe theorem can be written as\n\n$$\nc^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\color{red}c$ is the length of the longest side, which is called the **hypotenuse**, and\n- $\\color{blue}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are the lengths of the two other sides, which are called the **legs**."] [17592186069882 1029 "The **circumference** of a circle is the length of the path that goes around the circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/460/Source/Images/f1e77c0cd2f2322435cd574b1b205c04.png)\n\nThe circumference is usually denoted by the letter $C$.\n\nOne important fact about the circumference is that, for *any* circle, no matter how small or large it is, the ratio of circumference $C$ to diameter $d$ is a fixed number! Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{d} = {\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{\\pi}} \\approx 3.141\\,592\\,654..\n$$\n\nThis important irrational number is called **pi** (pronounced \"pie\") and we use the Greek letter $\\pi$ to express it. So, we can calculate the circumference by using the following formula:\n\n$$\nC = πd\n$$\n\nHere, our circle has diameter $4\\,\\textrm{cm}$, so its circumference is\n\n$$\nC = \\pi \\cdot 4 = 4\\pi \\approx 12.566\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal digits.\n\nSince the diameter is twice as long as the radius ($d=2r$), we can also write the formula for the circumference as\n\n$$\nC = \\pi d = 2\\pi r\n$$"] [17592186069883 1029 "If two limits *do not exist*, then we *cannot* say that they are equal because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nSo, if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x) = \\text{DNE}, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x \\to b} g(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x) \\neq \\lim\\limits_{x \\to b} g(x) \\,\n$$"] [17592186069884 1029 "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/d255ab82ce6f008296c3c7fe6a7c3b16.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/2e142a1ee145a3b329ede3e834b34111.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0$.\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0$. Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0$."] [17592186069885 1029 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **left-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^-} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[MATH: (-\\infty,2]]$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/1aade105a723ee84567208527881a116.png)\n\nNotice that at $x=2$, the left limit exists, while right limit does not exist because $f(x)$ is not defined for $x>2$.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4, \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nBecause the right-sided limit does not exist, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) = \\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2$. However, because the left-sided limit exists and is equal to the function value at $x=2$, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = 4 = f(2)\n$$\n\nwe can say that the function is **left-continuous** at $x=2$."] [17592186069886 1029 "A function $f(x)$ is said to be **right-continuous** at the point $x=c$ if the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^+} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[MATH: [-2,\\infty)]$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/474/Source/Images/8cdc9773c6fc461c78e751dff5d8a510.png)\n\nThe function is **right-continuous** at $x=-2$ because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2^+}f(x) = 4 = f(-2)\n$$"] [17592186069887 1029 "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0$, so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/462/Source/Images/3ea09156af8b143260f3c32e921edb31.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1$.\n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1$. Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1$.\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1$, we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1$."] [17592186069888 1029 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is said to be **continuous** at a point $x=c$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nIn order for the above statement to be true, we require both\n\n$$\nlim_(x → c^{-})f(x) = f(c)andlim_(x → c^{+})f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\nThe above equalities represent left-continuity and right-continuity. So, we have the following fact:\n\n> A function is **continuous** at a point if and only if it is *both* **left-continuous** *and* **right-continuous** at that point."] [17592186069889 1029 "The function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis undefined at $x=0$, but does that mean that the limit of this function at $x=0$ is also undefined?\n\nTo find out, let's start by looking at the graph of $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/473/Source/Images/33f7e50591d5203a8620a3ce3c3f4925.png)\n\nBased on the graph, it looks like $f(x) \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$. But let's try to find more convincing evidence.\n\nFirst, notice that the function $f(x)$ is squeezed (or sandwiched) between the two functions $y=x^2$ and $y=-x^2$. This happens because $\\sin(\\cdots)$ ranges between $-1$ and $1$, so $x^2 \\sin (\\cdots)$ ranges between $-x^2$ and $x^2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/473/Source/Images/ea70bff2b002f1f607e6f00559625513.png)\n\nStarting with\n\n$$\n-x^2 \\leq f(x) \\leq x^2\n$$\n\nwe take the limit as $x\\to 0$ and evaluate the two known limits, reaching\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}lim_(x → 0)(-x^{2}) \\le & lim_(x → 0)f(x) \\le lim_(x → 0)(x^{2}) \\\\ 0 \\le & lim_(x → 0)f(x) \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThere is only one number that satisfies the final inequality above, and that number is $0$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x) =0\n$$\n\nWe just applied the **squeeze theorem**, also called the **sandwich theorem**. Written generally, the squeeze theorem states that if a function $f(x)$ is squeezed between two functions $g(x)$ and $h(x)$, i.e.,\n\n$$\nh(x) \\le f(x) \\le g(x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} h(x)=\\lim_{x\\to a} g(x) =L\n$$\n\nfor constants $a$ and $L$, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) =L\n$$"] [17592186069890 1029 "What logarithmic equation is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nNotice that the given equation can be written using exponents as\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $a = b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_{b}{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2\n$$\n\nwith $a = b^x$, we get\n\n$$\na = 2, b = 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\log_{4}{2}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [17592186069891 1029 "When solving an equation, we sometimes require taking the square root of a negative number. In such cases, we will not end up with real solutions, but imaginary solutions.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2 +8 = 0\n$$\n\nfor $x$. First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x^2 + 8 &= 0 \\\\[3pt] 2x^2 &= -8 \\\\[3pt] x^2 &= -4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy taking the square root, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = \\pm \\sqrt{-4}\n$$\n\nWe now rewrite $x$ using the imaginary unit\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= ± \\sqrt{-4} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{4 \\cdot (-1)} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\\n&= ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i}\n$$"] [17592186069892 1029 "A **logarithm** (or log) is an operation that's the *inverse* of exponentiation.\n\nFor example, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8\n$$\n\nBut we can also write this using a logarithm as\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nIn words, we would say \"log of $8$ with base ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ equals ${\\color{red}{3}}$\". The **base** of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\n\nSince exponents and logarithms are opposite operations, an exponential equation can always be expressed in logarithmic form, and vice versa.\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with positive base\n\n$$\nb \\neq 1\n$$\n\nis defined as follows:\n\n> $a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}$."] [17592186069893 1029 "So far, we have expressed an exponential equation in logarithm form. We can also go the other way, and express a logarithmic equation in exponential form.\n\nRemember, the base of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\nFor instance, if we know that\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nwe could write this using an exponent as\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}$."] [17592186069894 1029 "We have learned how to factor quadratics. However, we can also factor higher-degree expressions, such as cubics. When factoring a cubic, the goal is exactly the same: we want to transform it into a product of two or three binomials.\n\nTo factor a cubic, we can often **factor by grouping**. To demonstrate, let's use this technique to factor the following cubic:\n\n$$\n3x^3-6x^2+2x-4\n$$\n\n**Step 1:** Group the two larger terms and the two smaller terms.\n\n$$\n(3x^3-6x^2)+(2x-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Factor the two larger terms and the two smaller terms separately, so that the leftover binomials inside the parentheses match. Here, we factor out $3x^2$ from the two larger terms and $2$ from the two smaller terms, and the leftover expressions $(x-2)$ match.\n\n$$\n3x^2(x -2)+2(x-2)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Rearrange the expression so that the binomial factors in parentheses appear at the front of each of the two terms.\n\n$$\n(x - 2)3x^2+(x-2)2\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Factor out the binomial in parentheses from the entire expression.\n\n$$\n(x - 2)(3x^2+2)\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! The cubic $3x^3-6x^2+2x-4$ factors into $(x - 2)(3x^2+2)$.\n\n**Note:** Just like with quadratics, not all cubics can be factored. However, of the cubics that can be factored, many can be factored by grouping."] [17592186069895 1029 "A polynomial of the form $a^3 + b^3$ is called a **sum of cubes**, while a polynomial of the form $a^3 – b^3$ is called a **difference of cubes**.\n\nBoth of these polynomials follow a similar factor pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{3}+b^{3} &= (a+b)(a^{2}-ab + b^{2}) \\\\\na^{3}-b^{3} &= (a-b)(a^{2}+ab + b^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOne trick to memorizing the signs in these patterns is to use the mnemonic **SOAP**, which stands for **S**ame, **O**pposite, **A**lways **P**ositive.\n\n- The sign of the linear factor is the **S**ame as that of the original polynomial,\n- the first sign of the quadratic factor is the **O**pposite sign, and\n- the last sign of the quadratic factor is **A**lways **P**ositive."] [17592186069896 1029 "A **root** of a polynomial is a value that will make the polynomial equal zero when substituted for the polynomial's variable.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $x^2-9$. This polynomial has two roots, $x=3$ and $x=-3$, because the polynomial evaluates to $0$ when either of these values is substituted for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx = 3⇒x^{2} - 9 &=  \\\\\n(3)^{2} - 9 &=  \\\\\n9 - 9 &=  \\\\\n0\\begin{vmatrix}✓ \\\\ x =-3⇒x^{2} - 9 &= \\\\ (-3)^{2} - 9|= \\\\ 9 - 9|= \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, a number like $x=1$ is *not* a root of the polynomial because the polynomial does not evaluate to $0$ when this value is substituted for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx = 1⇒x^{2} - 9 &=  \\\\\n(1)^{2} - 9 &=  \\\\\n1 - 9 &=  \\\\\n&= - 8 \\mid \\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069897 1029 "A **parametric polynomial** is a polynomial in which one of the coefficients is itself represented as another variable.\n\nFor example, the polynomial below is a parametric polynomial:\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x^2 -px\n$$\n\nHere, $p$ is a **parameter**, which means that it corresponds to a certain constant value. Unlike the variable $x$, the parameter is treated exactly like any other coefficient. It's only special in that we don't know its numerical value.\n\nIf we're given the root of a polynomial, we can find the numerical value of a parameter. Let's say, for instance, that a root of the above polynomial is $x=1$. This means that the polynomial is zero for that value:\n\n$$\nf(1)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=1$, set it equal to $0$, and solve the equation for the parameter $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(1) &= 0 \\\\\n2(1)^{2} - p(1) &= 0 \\\\\n2 - p &= 0 \\\\\np &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069898 1029 "A **quadratic inequality** is an inequality that involves a quadratic expression. We can often solve quadratic inequalities using a graphical method.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the solution to the quadratic inequality\n\n$$\nx^2+2x-3 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nLet's consider the graph of the corresponding parabola\n\n$$\ny=x^2+2x-3\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/468/Source/Images/347570866a30daee9b3485d6aa75860b.png)\n\nOur inequality is a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola is *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis\n\nUsing the graph above, we notice that our parabola has roots at $x=-3$ and $x=1$. The parabola is above the $x$-axis to the left of $x=-3$ and the right of $x=1$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/468/Source/Images/1d89c18e5862331348baf7a4b8c1ed16.png)\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\ny = x^2+2x-3 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x \\geq 1\n$$\n\nwhich is our solution.\n\n**Important:** Since we have a \"greater than *or equal to*\" inequality, the roots of the parabola are *included* in the solution set."] [17592186069899 1029 "When two fractions have the same denominator, we add the numerators. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 7 + \\dfrac 5 7 = \\dfrac{1+5}{7} = \\dfrac 6 7\n$$\n\nIn this case, both fractions were over a common denominator of $7$.\n\nIt works the same with rational expressions! So suppose that we want to add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x} + \\dfrac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nBecause both fractions have the same denominator of $x$, we can add the numerators as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x} + \\dfrac{5}{x} = \\dfrac{1+5}{x} = \\dfrac{6}{x}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!"] [17592186069900 1029 "Multiplying rational expressions is just like multiplying fractions. After all, fractions are just rational expressions with constants!\n\nFor example, to multiply the rational expressions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nwe multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\cdot \\dfrac{y}{2} = \\dfrac{3 \\cdot y}{x \\cdot 2} = \\dfrac{3y}{2x}\n$$"] [17592186069901 1029 "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nand to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\div \\frac{3}{y} &=  \\\\\nx \\cdot \\frac{y}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{xy}{3} \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!"] [17592186069902 1029 "The long division method allows us to divide a polynomial by another nonlinear polynomial.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}\n$$\n\nHere, the divisor is the quadratic polynomial $4x^2-1$.\n\nAgain, we follow a step-by-step process.\n\n**Step 1**\n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x + 4\n$$\n\n**Step 2**\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{-4x^3}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}})$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}{-4x^3}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-x}}\n$$\n\nWe add this result to the quotient line:\n$4x^{2} - 1- x; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x + 4$\n\nNext, we multiply the $\\color{#8b008b}-x$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\color{#8b008b}-x}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=-4x^3+x\n$$\n\nWe place this underneath the dividend, being sure to match the terms. Notice that we include a zero coefficient for the $x^2$ term.\n$4x^{2} - 1- x; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x + 4; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 0x^{2} + 0x_(―)$\n\nNow, we subtract $-4x^3+0x^2+x$ from $-4x^3+4x^2+x+4$ and get $4x^2+0x+4$:\n$4x^{2} - 1- x; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x + 4; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 0x^{2} + 0x_(―); 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} +4x^{2} + 0x + 4$\n\n**Step 3**\n\nWe now repeat the process. The new dividend is ${\\color{red}{4x^2}}+0x+4$.\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{4x^2}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}})$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{4x^2}}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{1}}\n$$\n\nWe add this result to the quotient:\n$4x^{2} - 1- x + 1; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x + 4; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 0x^{2} + 0x_(―); 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} +4x^{2} + 0x + 4$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\color{#8b008b}{1}}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=4x^2-1\n$$\n\nWe place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n4x^{2} - 1- x + 1; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x + 4; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 0x^{2} + 0x_(―); 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} +4x^{2} + 0x + 4; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} +4x^{2} + 0x - 1_(―)\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract $4x^2+0x-1$ from $4x^2+0x+4$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}$:\n$4x^{2} - 1- x + 1; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x + 4; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 0x^{2} + 0x_(―); 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} +4x^{2} + 0x + 4; 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} +4x^{2} + 0x - 1_(―); 4x^{2} - 1- 4x^{3} + 4x^{2} + 0x +5$\n\nWe're left with the constant $\\color{red}5$, which has a smaller degree than the divisor. So, the division is complete.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is ${\\color{blue}{-x+1}}$, and\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}$.\n\nOur complete result can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}={\\color{blue}{-x+1}} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{4x^2-1}\n$$"] [17592186069903 1029 "When considering the intersection of a line and a reciprocal function, three possibilities can occur.\n\nThe first possibility is that there are two intersection points, as illustrated in the example below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/a0f20a7b4a50be63fdffddf6ddc51d03.png)\n\nThe second possibility is that there is only one intersection point. For example:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/bf5108342500a3e90de1407c461312fd.png)\n\nThe final possibility is that there are no intersection points. For example:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/806c5a704d7a0c5ecce1daa1bb924cf2.png)\n\nTypically, when computing the points of intersection of a reciprocal function and a line, we have to solve a system of two equations. In the first graph that was shown above, this system was\n\n$$\n{y = x; y = \\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nThis system of equations can (usually) be transformed into a single rational equation, which can itself be converted into a quadratic equation. For the above system, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{x} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x^2 = 1\n$$\n\nBy solving the quadratic equation, we can find the intersection points. And like quadratic equations, there can be two, one, or no real solutions. In this case, there are two solutions:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = \\pm 1\n$$"] [17592186069904 1029 "Suppose we want to find the points where the line $y=x-2$ intersects the radical function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x+4}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph below, we can see that there is one point of intersection. Let's calculate its coordinates!\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/411/Source/Images/82e672086a0cf8bba101076cba0640e4.png)\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations $y=x-2$ and\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x+4}\n$$\n\nIn other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = \\sqrt{x + 4}; y = x - 2\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x + 4} &= x - 2 \\\\\nx + 4 &= (x - 2)^{2} \\\\\nx + 4 &= x^{2} - 4x + 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we're dealing with square roots, we need to plug each of these solutions back into\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2\n$$\n\nto check whether it satisfies the original equation.\n\n- For $x=0$, we have\n$\\sqrt{0 + 4}|= 0 - 2; 2|=-2, \\cdot$\nso $x=0$ is not a solution of $\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2$.\n- For $x=5$, we have\n$\\sqrt{5 + 4}|= 5 - 2; 3|= 3, ✓$\nso $x=5$ is indeed a solution of $\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2$.\n\nWe found that $x=5$ is the only solution of\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+4}=x-2\n$$\n\nWe now calculate the $y$-coordinate of the intersection by plugging $x=5$ in one of the two original curves. Let's choose the line, since it's simpler:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x - 2 \\\\\n&= 5 - 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point of intersection is $(5,3)$.\n\nLet's label this point in our sketch:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/411/Source/Images/31ddb0454f9caac7b43e56a995cb9fec.png)"] [17592186069905 1029 "To solve an equation that contains multiple rational terms, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator.\n\nFor example, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2x^2}= \\dfrac{1}{2x}-\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution because it would make the denominators equal zero.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $2x^2$, $2x$, and $x$, so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(2x^2, 2x, x) = {\\color{black}2x^2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by ${\\color{black}2x^2}$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x^{2}} &= 2x^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2x} - \\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n2x^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x^{2}} &= \\frac{2x^{2}}{2x} - \\frac{2x^{2}}{x} \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{2x \\cdot x}{2x} - \\frac{x \\cdot 2x}{x} \\\\\n1 &= x - 2x \\\\\n1 &= -x \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x = -1$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$."] [17592186069906 1029 "To sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{x}\n$$\n\nwe start with the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nand stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/455/Source/Images/2936abad3acbc10246cdae3489fdb5fc.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The point $(1,1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $(1,2)$ on $y=\\dfrac2x$.\n- The point $(-1,-1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $(-1,-2)$ on $y=\\dfrac2x$."] [17592186069907 1029 "We can find which values satisfy an inequality by trial and error, which involves substituting a number for the variable and checking to see if we get a true statement.\n\nFor example, which of the following values of $y$ satisfy the inequality $y - 1 > 3\\,$?\n\n1. $y=6$\n2. $y=2$\n3. $y=3$\n\nLet's go through the possibilities one at a time.\n\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 6 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 5 & > 3✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **true** statement, so $y=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 2 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 1 & > 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n- Substituting the value ${\\color{blue}y}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given equation, we get:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 3 - 1 & > 3 \\\\ 2 & > 3\\cdot\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis is a **false** statement, so $y=3$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\nTherefore, only I satisfies the inequality."] [17592186069908 1029 "Adding a value to $y = x^2$ will **translate** (shift) the parabola up or down the $y$-axis. For instance, adding $\\color{blue}2$ to $y = x^2$ will move each point on the parabola $2$ units up.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/453/Source/Images/8fcca0acc23374607bff6fddf92c0fed.png)\n\nOn the other hand, subtracting $\\color{red}3$ will move each point of the parabola $3$ units down.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/453/Source/Images/8612494a1f66e75af427df874f31c9b3.png)\n\nIn general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nlooks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of\n\n$$\ny=x^2+k\n$$\n\nis located at $(0,k)$."] [17592186069909 1029 "Whether the left Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/477/Source/Images/05800258a79f954305aaa47c9479df31.png)\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/477/Source/Images/85a6d9e1be51f841426a88bd38173231.png)"] [17592186069910 1029 "In this lesson, we started out with a special case of L'Hopital's rule (the case of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nas $x \\to a$) and gradually introduced more cases. In closing, we will state the full version of L'Hopital's rule.\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\dfrac{0}{0} \\quad \\textrm{or} \\quad \\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\dfrac{\\pm \\infty}{\\pm \\infty}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any real number, or $\\pm \\infty$. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n$$\n\nprovided that the following conditions are met:\n\n- If $a$ is a real number, and $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a$, then the limit $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n- If $a$ is $\\pm \\infty$, and $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable for all sufficiently large $x$, then the limit $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n\nWhen working with familiar functions like polynomials, trigonometric functions, exponential functions, logarithmic functions, etc, it's safe to assume that these conditions are met."] [17592186069911 1029 "Let's now turn our attention to the cases where a line and a reciprocal function intersect at either one point or no points.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find where the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}+1\n$$\n\nand the line $y=2$ intersect. To do this, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{x} + 1; y = 2\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and solving for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x} + 1 &= 2 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the corresponding $y$-coordinate, we plug $x=1$ into either equation. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{x} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1} + 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)\n$$\n\n.\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/434/Source/Images/f8c6d88a4b4650e683579678cb4e54fe.png)"] [17592186069912 1029 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nNotice that we included both positive and negative input values since the cube root is defined over all real numbers.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/454/Source/Images/d6b5ce73323df0222a37c8d170e707b9.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$.\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- Its range is $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty$, though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n- As $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow -\\infty$, though the function decreases more slowly as $x$ becomes very negative.\n\nIt is also worth noting that\n\n$$\nf(x) =\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nis an invertible function, and its inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = x^3, \\qquad x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nand its inverse are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/454/Source/Images/6b630eefc585e2c752f2b15df56ee588.png)"] [17592186069913 1029 "The intersection points between the absolute value graph\n\n$$\ny= \\mid ax+b \\mid\n$$\n\nand the line\n\n$$\ny=cx+d\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the solutions of the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid ax+b &= cx+d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDepending on the positions of the respective graphs, there are typically two, one, or no solutions to this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/385/Source/Images/03f281c2d791755ff0f38cf8e3f6d0d9.png)\n\nFor the remainder of this lesson, we'll consider only the cases where two intersection points exist between the absolute value function and the line. Cases with only one or no intersection points will be dealt with in a future lesson."] [17592186069914 1029 "Similarly, to sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2x}\n$$\n\nwe take the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nand stretch it by a scale factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nparallel to the $y$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/455/Source/Images/9c30a8687e5659b219ce739afbeb756e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The point $(1,1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $\\left(1,\\dfrac12\\right)$ on $y=\\dfrac{1}{2x}$.\n- The point $(-1,-1)$ on $y=\\dfrac1x$ is mapped to the point $\\left(-1,-\\dfrac12\\right)$ on $y=\\dfrac{1}{2x}$."] [17592186069915 1029 "We can apply stretches to negative reciprocal curves too.\n\nFor example, to sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{3}{2x}\n$$\n\nwe start with the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nand stretch it by a scale factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac32\n$$\n\nparallel to the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/455/Source/Images/c0bc43d4a33b0aa90dbc10c8a45d03c1.png)"] [17592186069916 1029 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $x=f(y)$, the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$, as shown below. If we rotate this region $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a solid of revolution.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/415/Source/Images/35c18ba0dac4863c744b7efe5887d595.png)\n\nOne way of computing the volume of the solid is to divide the interval from $y=c$ to $y=d$ on the $y$-axis to get horizontal slices. This approach is called the **shell method**.\n\nConsider one such slice of height $\\Delta{y}$, as shown in the picture below (on the left). When this rectangle is rotated about the $x$-axis, it forms a solid **shell** (i.e., the skin of a cylinder).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/415/Source/Images/7964fceb743db12f3a099173433af984.png)\n\nSince we assume that $\\Delta{y}$ is very small, the outer radius of the shell approximately equals the inner radius $y$. The thickness of the shell is $\\Delta{y}$ and its height is $x=f(y)$.\n\nImagine we cut this shell and unfold it to obtain a rectangular solid, as shown below. Its width is $x=f(y)$, its thickness is $\\Delta{y}$, and its height is $2\\pi y$ (the circumference of the shell).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/415/Source/Images/cdf6ac0d0fe6a6a1b2b5f62dcf34e058.png)\n\nThe volume of the shell approximately equals the volume of the rectangular solid:\n\n$$\nV_{shell} \\mid \\approx V_{rec}; = 2πy \\cdot x \\cdot Δy; = 2πyf(y)Δy\n$$\n\nThe sum over all the shells will approximate the total volume that we want. This approximation becomes more accurate as $\\Delta{y} \\to 0$. Therefore, the volume $V$ of the solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π∫_{c}^{d}yxdy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{c}^{d}yf(y)dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069917 1029 "One of the advantages of using the shell method versus other methods is that the resulting integral can be much simpler to evaluate. This is often true when we want to rotate a curve of the form $x = f(y)$ about the $x$-axis.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the volume generated when the region below is rotated about the $x$-axis:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/415/Source/Images/aec37a99eb9deabf48ae2263390e2504.png)\n\nAccording to the shell method, the volume $V$ is given by the following straightforward integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2π∫_{0}^{1}yf(y)dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}y(2y - 2y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}2y^{2} - 2y^{3}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now compare this to the disc method.\n\nTo determine this volume using the disc method, we'd first need to express our curve as two separate functions of $x$, one for the lower part and one for the upper part, as follows:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2x},\\begin{vmatrix}y \\ge \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1 - 2x},\\end{vmatrix}y < \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/415/Source/Images/d80785f843ff765556eb519a35c1fd40.png)\n\nThe required volume is equal to the difference between the volumes generated when the upper and lower curves are rotated about the $x$-axis. Thus, according to the disc method, the required volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{0}^{1/2} \\left(\\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 - \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1-2x} \\right]^2 \\right) \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs we can see, this integral is a lot more complicated than the one we obtained using the shell method!"] [17592186069918 1029 "Remember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nwe need to put the fractions over a **common denominator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt works the same in algebraic rational expressions! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators."] [17592186069919 1029 "Suppose that we want to find\n\n$$\n{\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\frac {\\sin x}{x}}\n$$\n\nIf we try to plug in $x=0$, we get the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo what can we do?\n\nFortunately, we can find this limit using **L'Hopital's Rule**, which states that if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x) = 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x) = 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\ \\lim_{x\\to a} \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a$, and that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a} \\dfrac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n$$\n\nexists.\n\nSo, by applying L'Hopital's rule to our limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac {\\sin x}{x} &= \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {[\\sin x]'}{[x]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac {\\cos x}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\cos 0 \\\\[5pt] &= 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069920 1029 "We can also use L'Hopital's rule to deal with the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\n. In this case, L'Hopital's rule states that if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x) = \\pm\\infty\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x) = \\pm\\infty\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a$, and the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n$$\n\nexists."] [17592186069921 1029 "L'Hopital's rule can be extended to the case in which the limit is taken as $x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty$. In this case, L'Hopital's rule states that if either\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty}f(x) = \\pm \\infty$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty}g(x) = \\pm \\infty$, or\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty}f(x) = 0$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty}g(x) = 0$,\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty} \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable for all sufficiently large $x$, and the limit\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty} \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}\n$$\n\nexists."] [17592186069922 1029 "Suppose we want to find the solution to the quadratic inequality\n$x^2+x-6 < 0$.\n\nWe first plot the graph of the corresponding parabola\n\n$$\ny=x^2+x-6\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/468/Source/Images/13e601f52ad1c4eb14d1c0b194284441.png)\n\nNotice that our inequality is a \"*less than*\" ($<$) inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola is *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nThe graph above shows that our parabola has roots at $x=-3$ and $x=2$. Moreover, the parabola lies below the $x$-axis for all values of $x$ that lie between $x=-3$ and $x=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/468/Source/Images/70ea9357f1832f7d643f8f714ddff677.png)\n\nTherefore, $x^2+x-6 <0$ when\n$-3 < x < 2$,\n\nwhich is our solution.\n\n**Important:** Since our inequality is a \"*less than*\" inequality, the roots of the parabola are *not* included in the solution set."] [17592186069923 1029 "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + {\\color{blue}{a}}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNot all quadratic expressions are perfect squares. However, every quadratic expression can be expressed as a perfect square *plus a constant*. In this lesson, we'll learn how to write quadratic expressions in this form.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 6x + 3^2\\right) + 4\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 6x + 3^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n$(x + 3)^{2} + 4$.\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully written the original expression as a perfect square $(x + {\\color{blue}3})^2$ plus a constant $(4)$.\n\nWriting a quadratic expression as a perfect square plus a constant is called **completing the square.** Completing the square is a valuable technique for solving many problems.\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186069924 1029 "Given a quadratic equation in the form\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwe can find its roots using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this formula, the expression under the square root is called the **discriminant**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = {\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}\n$$\n\nThe value of the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ tells us a lot about the solutions of our quadratic equation.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}>0$, then we get $2$ distinct real solutions: one with the positive square root and one with the negative square root.\n- If $\\mathcal{D}=0$, then we get $1$ real solution because the square root vanishes. We sometimes say that the equation has two equal solutions.\n- If $\\mathcal{D}<0$, then we get no real solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples:\n\n- If $x^2+3x+2=0$, then we have a positive discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=3^2-4(1)(2) = 1$ and $2$ distinct real solutions:\n$x = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2-4(1)(2)}}{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{1}}{2} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm 1}{2} = -2,-1$\n- If $x^2+2x+1=0$, then we have a zero discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=2^2-4(1)(1) = 0$ and $1$ distinct real solution:\n$x = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(1)}}{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{0}}{2} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 0}{2} = -1$\n- If $x^2+x+1=0$, then we have a negative discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=1^2-4(1)(1) = -3$ and no real solutions:\n$x = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(1)}}{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}$."] [17592186069925 1029 "Suppose we have the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- the coefficients $a$, $b$, and $c$ are all *rational numbers,* and\n- the discriminant is non-negative, i.e., $\\mathcal D \\geq 0$.\n\nThen, we have the following possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is a *perfect square*, then the equation has two *rational* solutions.\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is *not* a perfect square, then the equation has two *irrational* solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples.\n\nConsider the equation $x^2-x-6=0$. Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= (-1)^{2} - 4(1)(-6) \\\\\n&= 1 + 24 \\\\\n&= 25 \\\\\n&= 5^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a perfect square. Consequently, the corresponding solutions are both rational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{5^2}}{2(1)} = 3, -2\n$$\n\nNow, consider the equation $x^2-x-5=0$. Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= (-1)^{2} - 4(1)(-5) \\\\\n&= 1 + 20 \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a perfect square. Consequently, the solutions are both irrational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}\n$$"] [17592186069926 1029 "To simplify radical expressions, we can use the quotient rule for radicals, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\sqrt {a}} {\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac a b}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's simplify\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{16w^2}{25}} = \\dfrac {\\sqrt{16w^2}} {\\color{blue}\\sqrt{25}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5^2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{16w^2}}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression under the radical as a perfect square and taking its square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{16w^{2}}}{5} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{(4w)^{2}})/(5) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{\\mid 4w\\begin{vmatrix}}{5} &= \\\\ \\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}4 & \\cdot & w\\end{vmatrix}}{5}|= \\\\ \\frac{4 \\cdot \\mid w \\mid}{5}|= \\\\ \\frac{4 \\mid w\\end{vmatrix}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069927 1029 "The quotient rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac a b} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{a}}{\\sqrt[3]{b}}\n$$\n\nIn general, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{\\dfrac a b} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt[n]{a}}{\\sqrt[n]{b}}\n$$"] [17592186069928 1029 "Sometimes, we must solve an absolute value equation containing variables on both sides. For example,\n\n$$\n\\left\\vert 5x +7\\right\\vert = 3x +9\n$$\n\nGeometrically, these solutions correspond to the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points of the curves\n\n$$\ny= \\mid 5x+7 \\mid\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=3x+9\n$$\n\nshown as the points $P$ and $Q$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/385/Source/Images/6b717ad6792087d8a375388e2b3b4b5f.png)\n\n**Note:** *Image not drawn to scale.*\n\nLet's now discuss how to find these intersection points algebraically."] [17592186069929 1029 "We wish to find the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\vert 5x + 7 \\vert = 3x +9\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nTherefore, we have the following two cases:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}5x+7 & = 5x+7 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad & 5x+7\\end{vmatrix} = -(5x+7)\n$$\n\nWe now consider these two cases separately:\n\n- If $\\mid 5x +7|=5x +7$, we obtain the following equation:\n$5x +7 = 3x +9$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$\\begin{array}{rl}5 x + 7 & = 3 x + 9 \\\\ 2 x + 7 & = 9 \\\\ 2 x & = 2 \\\\ x & = 1\\end{array}$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 5x + 7 \\mid |= 3x + 9; \\mid 5(1) + 7 \\mid |= 3(1) + 9; \\mid 5 + 7 \\mid |= 3 + 9; \\mid 12 \\mid |= 12; 12|= 12✓$\nSince $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 5x +7|=-(5x +7)$, we obtain the following equation:\n$-(5x +7)=3x +9$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$- (5x + 7)|= 3x + 9; - 5x - 7|= 3x + 9; - 7|= 8x + 9; - 16|= 8x; - 2|= x; x|=-2$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$\\mid 5x + 7 \\mid |= 3(-2) + 9; \\mid 5(-2) + 7 \\mid |= 3(-2) + 9; \\mid - 10 + 7 \\mid |=-6 + 9; \\mid - 3 \\mid |= 3; 3|= 3✓$\nSince $x=-2$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of our equation are $x=-2, \\, 1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/385/Source/Images/c084b7ef5e4e21e77d0580530d6c1d10.png)"] [17592186069930 1029 "A line that crosses two parallel lines is called a **transversal**. For example, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ in the diagram below is a transversal.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/421/Source/Images/37a2f948e13ab3aa5d81709aa259f2cf.png)\n\nA pair of parallel lines with a transversal form a set of $8$ angles, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/421/Source/Images/452b735fdfc2cf5e75d3183d3e2266d1.png)\n\nThis set of eight angles can be grouped into two categories:\n\n- We have four pairs of **vertical angles**:![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/421/Source/Images/3a1ba69f66bfa7303cef512c22028569.png)\n\n- We also have four pairs of so-called **corresponding angles**:![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/421/Source/Images/c4fde7476233a3662c3451e6df11c0f7.png)\nCorresponding pairs of angles are located in the same position as each other relative to the parallel lines and on the same side of the transversal. Pairs of corresponding angles are congruent."] [17592186069931 1029 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x - y}\\,\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of both $x$ and $y$, so we can't solve the equation by integrating both sides with respect to $x$, at least not straight away. So, how do we find a general solution to the equation?\n\nFirst of all, note that it is possible to separate the variables $x$ and $y$ on each side of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x - y} = \\frac{e^{x}}{e^{y}}\\Longrightarrow e^{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int e^{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int e^{x}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the rule for integration by substitution, we can simplify the integrand on the left-hand side and carry out the integration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int e^{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int e^{x}dx \\\\\n\\int e^{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int e^{x}dx \\\\\n\\int e^{y}dy &= \\int e^{x}dx \\\\\ne^{y} &= e^{x} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ne^y = e^x + C\n$$\n\nThis is an **implicit form** of the solution since it is not written explicitly as $y=y(x)$.\n\nWe can write our general solution in **explicit form** (i.e., in the form $y=y(x)$) by noting that since $e^y > 0$ for all $y$, we may assume that the right-hand side is positive and apply the natural logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (e^{y}) &= \\ln (e^{x} + C) \\\\\ny &= \\ln (e^{x} + C)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069932 1029 "The differential equation that we just solved was **separable**. A first-order differential equation is separable if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y)\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)\n$$\n\nOnce the variables are separated, we can integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\int  \\frac{1}{g ( y )} \\cdot  \\frac{\\text{d} y}{\\text{d} x} \\, \\text{d} x & = \\int  h ( x ) \\, \\text{d} x \\\\ \\int  \\frac{\\text{d} y}{g ( y )} & = \\int  h ( x ) \\, \\text{d} x .\\end{array}$$\n\nWe then evaluate both integrals to obtain an implicit relation. This division-and-integration procedure is valid on intervals where\n$$g(y)\\ne0.$$\nWe must also check roots $y=c$ of $g(c)=0$ in the original equation: each such constant function is an equilibrium solution wherever the equation is defined. Division by $g(y)$ would otherwise lose these solutions."] [17592186069933 1029 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/477/Source/Images/b923340927f80192d4432c9e4a6d659c.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/477/Source/Images/f9d2c3158eb9a2d8d20544aab131091f.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(1)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(2)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle) + [f(3)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]3[/math]rd rectangle); = [1^{2} \\cdot 1] + [2^{2} \\cdot 1] + [3^{2} \\cdot 1]; = 1 + 4 + 9; = 14\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4]$, the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area."] [17592186069934 1029 "In general, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ can be approximated using a left Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/477/Source/Images/01bbea4655115ee6aea7c1844960a765.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(x_0)\\Delta x+f(x_1)\\Delta x+f(x_2)\\Delta x+ \\cdots + f(x_{n-1}) \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the $\\Delta x$, which gives the following general formula:\n\n$$\nheights\n$$"] [17592186069935 1029 "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4]. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/477/Source/Images/0415c229ccd591643da24a7acbe5823c.png)\n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{A} &\\approx {\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1}) + {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2}) + {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} \\\\[5pt] &=1+6+3 \\\\[5pt] &= 10. \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186069936 1029 "Consider the following pair of parallel lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, and the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/421/Source/Images/a31c586fdf18c426fb2c1705f858c632.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $x$?\n\nFirst, note that $\\angle DIF$ and $\\angle BJI$ are corresponding angles. Therefore, they are congruent and thus have the same measure. This means we can write down the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DIF &= m∠BJI \\\\\n3x - 20^{∘} &= x + 10^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first subtract $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 20^{∘} &= x + 10^{∘} \\\\\n3x - 20^{∘} - x &= x + 10^{∘} - x \\\\\n2x - 20^{∘} &= 10^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $20^\\circ$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 20^{∘} &= 10^{∘} \\\\\n2x - 20^{∘} + 20^{∘} &= 10^{∘} + 20^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 30^{∘} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{30^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= 15^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 15^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=15^\\circ$."] [17592186069937 1029 "Suppose that we have the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx(t) = t+1, \\qquad y(t) = t^2+2, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThese parametric equations trace out a curve in the $xy$-plane. We can combine these into a single function, called a **vector-valued** function $\\mathbf r (t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t)\\rangle = \\langle t+1, \\: t^2+2\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf r (t)$ takes a **parameter** $t$ as input and returns a **position vector**. For a given value of $t$, each value of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has its tail at the origin and its terminal point at the coordinates\n$(t+1, \\: t^2+2)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/475/Source/Images/120d628591c59230fd13d070ea864490.png)\n\nEvaluating vector-valued functions is straightforward. Just remember that the output is a vector, not a scalar.\n\nFor the vectors in the graph above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(-3) &= ⟨(-3) + 1, (-3)^{2} + 2⟩ = ⟨ - 2, 11⟩, \\\\\nr(0) &= ⟨(0) + 1, (0)^{2} + 2⟩ = ⟨1, 2⟩, \\\\\nr(2) &= ⟨(2) + 1, (2)^{2} + 2⟩ = ⟨3, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
